{
  "result": "I've been thinking lately about how much we miss when we're in a hurry. Not the big things \u2014 sunsets and birthdays and trips \u2014 but the small, unremarkable moments that quietly hold a day together.\n\nThis morning I watched light move across my wall. It started as a thin gold line through the blinds, then widened, softened, spilled. Nothing spectacular. No one would photograph it. But I sat there with coffee going lukewarm in my hands and felt strangely lucky to witness it.\n\nI think curiosity is a form of kindness \u2014 toward the world and toward ourselves. When you pause to notice the way rain sounds different on leaves versus umbrellas, or how a friend's laugh changes when they're truly relaxed, you're saying: this matters, you matter, I'm here.\n\nWe live in a time that rewards speed and certainty, hot takes and quick answers. But slowness has its own intelligence. A soup tastes better when it simmers. A thought deepens when you let it wander. A friendship grows in the unhurried spaces between plans.\n\nSo here's my small wish for today, for you and for me: notice one tiny thing you'd normally rush past. The chipped blue mug at the back of the shelf. The neighbor's cat patrolling like a tiny mayor. The breath you take before answering a hard question.\n\nLife isn't only in the milestones. It's in the light moving across the wall, waiting \u2014 patiently, beautifully \u2014 for someone to look up and truly see it.",
